<title>CANADA: No frost seen on Canada's Prairies Friday - Environment Canada.</title>
<headline>No frost seen on Canada's Prairies Friday - Environment Canada.</headline>
<dateline>WINNIPEG 1997-08-15</dateline>
<text>
<p>No frost was reported anywhere on Canada's Prairie grain belt Friday morning, an Environment Canada meteorologist said.</p>
<p>&quot;It looks like across the Prairies most of the temperatures stayed in the double digits, we didn't get frost anywhere,&quot; meteorologist Henry Schmidt said.</p>
<p>The coldest overnight lows forecast for the next five days were expected in southern Manitoba Sunday and Monday mornings at 4.0 Celsius (39 F), Schmidt said.</p>
<p>Prairie temperatures were forecast to rise Monday and Tuesday as a warm air mass comes from the south, he added.</p>
<p>Normal lows at this time of year range from 9.0 to 11 Celsius and the normal high is 25 Celsius.</p>
<p>((Gilbert Le Gras, Winnipeg Reuters bureau 204 947 3548))</p>
